  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nHigh pressure centered over the Great Lakes will keep a dry and \ncooler airmass in place throughout the day on Monday. By Tuesday, the\nhigh begins to shift east, bringing a return of southerly flow and a\nwarming trend. Temperatures on Tuesday rise into the upper 80s, \nwhile low 90s are forecast for Wednesday. \n\nA short wave will track from the northern Plains across the upper \nGreat Lakes, pushing a cold front into the area Wednesday into\nThursday. Given how far removed ILN is from the warm sector of the\nsystem, moisture ahead of the front isn't robust. However, as the\nfront slowly sags down into the region, it looks to nearly stall out\n(or be very slow to progress) and PWATs along the front are forecast\nto be around 200% of normal. While, at this junction, severe weather\ndoesn't look overly likely, any training storms along the slow moving\nboundary would introduce a flash flooding threat. \n\nWhile exact timing on FROPA is a bit ambiguous, ensembles suggest \nthat it will be passing through the FA on Thursday. Once this front \npasses through the region, cooler and drier air will once again \nfilter in for the end of the work week.",
